Microfabrication and material integration — MIT 6.777J, 2007 (@mat-mit-microfab: 0, is #reference, #part-of @mat-sources) { r_1F4QYJ2V4BZ2T9YXX6Q35J6KFT
Carol Livermore and Joel Voldman. *Design and Fabrication of Microelectromechanical Devices*, Spring 2007, graduate MIT OpenCourseWare. [Lecture sequence](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-777j-design-and-fabrication-of-microelectromechanical-devices-spring-2007/pages/lecture-notes/), [assignments](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-777j-design-and-fabrication-of-microelectromechanical-devices-spring-2007/pages/assignments/), [projects](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-777j-design-and-fabrication-of-microelectromechanical-devices-spring-2007/pages/projects/).

Read lectures 1–5 in full for process integration, patterning, deposition, etching and material state; revisit mechanics/thermal lectures and lecture 19 packaging for the advanced layered-material study. Selected thin-film and etching passages of lectures 2 and 3 were inspected on 30 October 2026: 56 and 65 PDF pages respectively. Process examples describe their historical context, not universally current fabrication specifications. Some figures are explicitly omitted or separately credited.

MIT OCW uses CC BY-NC-SA with exceptions. Preserve slide-level credits, distinguish the commercial textbook from the public notes, and keep readings and media external. The complete semiconductor/device and MEMS subjects remain separate deeper continuations.

**Return:** [Materials-engineering sources — reading and primary evidence](../Sources.lingua#r_51JAH6DB4WV2HZAB69G21ZHSMK).
} r_1F4QYJ2V4BZ2T9YXX6Q35J6KFT
